= Sparse Demand Models {#ch:method}

Freight demand between regions is typically modelled as a dense origin–destination
matrix \cite{ortuzar2011}. In practice, however, fewer than 5\% of region pairs
carry 80\% of volume \cite[p.~214]{daganzo2005}, which suggests a sparse
representation.\ix{demand matrix!sparse} This chapter develops such a model and
derives its estimator.

== Problem Formulation {#sec:formulation}

Let $\mathcal{R} = \{1, \dots, n\}$ be the set of regions and let
$D \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{n \times n}$ denote the demand matrix, where $D_{ij}$
is the volume shipped from region $i$ to region $j$.\ix{demand matrix} We observe
noisy link counts $y \in \mathbb{R}^m$ related to $D$ through a known assignment
matrix $A$:

$$
  y = A \operatorname{vec}(D) + \varepsilon,
  \qquad \varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I)
$$ {#eq:obs}

Following \textcite{tibshirani1996}, we estimate $D$ by penalised least squares:

$$
  \hat{D} = \argmin_{D \ge 0}\;
    \frac{1}{2} \lVert y - A \operatorname{vec}(D) \rVert_2^2
    + \lambda \lVert \operatorname{vec}(D) \rVert_1
$$ {#eq:lasso}

where $\lambda > 0$ controls sparsity. Equation @eq:lasso reduces to @eq:obs in
the maximum-likelihood sense when $\lambda \to 0$.

\theorem[id=thm:unique, name="Uniqueness"]{
  If the columns of $A$ are in general position, the solution of @eq:lasso is
  unique for every $\lambda > 0$.
}

\proof{
  The objective is strictly convex on the affine hull of the active set; see
  \cite[Lemma~2]{tibshirani2013} for the general argument.
}

== Estimation {#sec:estimation}

We solve @eq:lasso by coordinate descent. The update for a single entry
$d_k$ has the closed form

$$\begin{aligned}
  r_k   &= y - \sum_{l \ne k} a_l d_l, \\
  d_k   &\leftarrow \frac{\max\!\left(0,\; a_k^\top r_k - \lambda\right)}{\lVert a_k \rVert_2^2}.
\end{aligned}$$ {#eq:update}

\figure[id=fig:convergence]{
  \subfigure[id=fig:conv-a, width=48%]{
    \image{figures/convergence-small.png}
    \caption{$n = 50$}
  }
  \subfigure[id=fig:conv-b, width=48%]{
    \image{figures/convergence-large.png}
    \caption{$n = 400$}
  }
  \caption{Objective value per iteration of the update @eq:update.}
}

@fig:convergence shows that convergence is reached within 40 iterations for
both problem sizes (@fig:conv-a, @fig:conv-b).\footnote{All experiments ran on a
single CPU core; timings are reported in Table @tbl:timing.}

```python {#lst:cd, caption="Coordinate descent for the sparse demand estimator"}
def coordinate_descent(A, y, lam, iters=100):
    d = np.zeros(A.shape[1])
    col_norm2 = (A ** 2).sum(axis=0)
    for _ in range(iters):
        for k in range(len(d)):
            r = y - A @ d + A[:, k] * d[k]
            d[k] = max(0.0, A[:, k] @ r - lam) / col_norm2[k]
    return d
```

\table[style="Thesis Table", header-rows=1, widths=(1fr, 3cm, 3cm), id=tbl:timing,
       columns=(left, decimal, decimal)]{
  | Regions $n$ | Time (s) | Iterations |
  | 50          | 0.42     | 31         |
  | 400         | 37.9     | 38         |
}
\caption[@tbl:timing, above]{Run time of Listing @lst:cd.}

== Summary

This chapter introduced the sparse demand model @eq:lasso, proved uniqueness of
its solution (Theorem @thm:unique) and gave an efficient estimator. Chapter
@ch:experiments evaluates it on national freight data.
